    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n\r\nThis adds support for intuitively navigating through tabpages using the same idea of `[` and `]` mappings as part of mini.bracketed. The suffix for tabpages that I've considered is the `<tab>` key itself.",
    "closed_at": "2024-01-06T08:01:56Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thank you for your time doing this!\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this is out of scope because there already are built-in mappings for navigating tabpages: `gt` and `gT`. If user wants to have 'mini.bracketed' style of navigation, there is this set of mappings:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '[T', '<Cmd>tabfirst<CR>')\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '[t', '<Cmd>tabprevious<CR>')\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', ']t', '<Cmd>tabnext<CR>')\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', ']T', '<Cmd>tablast<CR>')\r\n```\r\n\r\nThose don't really support `{count}` but should be good enough alternative. Using `<Tab>` as a key instead of `t` is a lot less ergonomic than using `gt` / `gT` in my opinion, as `[` / `]` and `<Tab>` are (usually) quite literally on the opposite sides of the keyboard. But `t` is used for tree-sitter, so there is a conflict here.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nPlease, open an issue to discuss the change first. This might save you much time.",
            "created_at": "2024-01-06T08:01:56Z",
